WEBVTT

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.640
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:21.320
 In this fifth season, we're speaking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists about migrations of all kinds.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:31.500
 We'll hear about food and the experience of leaving home and in finding new ones, of decolonizing food traditions, and tracing recipes through the movements of diaspora.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.640
 Delicious Revolution is made by Devon Samson and Chelsea Wills.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.730
 You can subscribe to the show on Apple Podcasts or wherever you get your podcasts.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:48.290
 And you can find all of our episodes along with pictures and more on the website deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:01:03.320
 Maya Shiroyama and Jim Ryugo run Kitazawa Seeds, a hundred-year-old seed distributor based in California that specializes in Asian vegetables.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:10.800
 The company was started by Giju Kitazawa in 1917, serving mostly Japanese-American gardeners on the West Coast.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:23.240
 It closed for four years when the United States government sent Kitazawa and most of his customers to internment camps during World War II, and reopened in 1945 shortly after their release.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:34.380
 Maya and Jim had planted the company seeds in their home garden for years, when a missing seed order led them to buying the seed company from Kitazawa's granddaughter in 2000.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:50.770
 In this episode, Maya and Jim talk with Evan about the 100-year history of the seed company, the global network of growers that supply their unique inventory, and the new and old varieties dear to Asian-American gardeners and farmers around the country.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:02:04.330
 My name is Maya Shiroyama, and I'm with Kitazawa Seed Company, and my husband.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:08.980
 Hi, I'm Jim Ryugo, co-owner of Kitazawa Seed Company.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:13.370
 Hey, thanks so much for making some time to talk to me, and welcome to Delicious Revolution.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:20.970
 I guess I wanted to start with the fact that this business has been around for 100 years this year.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:24.190
 Can you give me the history of the company?

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:34.490
 Kitazawa Seed Company started in 1917 by an immigrant who came to the United States probably in the early 1900s.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:46.250
 He settled in the San Jose area, and he had been trained in Japan in, I guess, by a seed company.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.610
 He was some sort of apprentice in Japan.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:53.830
 So he knew a little bit about seeds, or maybe knew a lot about seeds, I don't know.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:03:08.710
 He came to America, he set down his roots, and he realized that there were a lot of other immigrants from Japan who were longing for a taste of their homeland.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:25.310
 And he started importing seeds from Japan and selling them from basically the back of his garage type of thing to other fellow immigrants up and down the coast and into the Central Valley.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:33.370
 And back in the early 1900s, you literally went door-to-door, farmer-to-farmer selling your seeds.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:35.630
 It was a whole different world back then.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:48.340
 And so the sale of seeds were based upon personal relationships with other farmers, word of mouth, family connections, relatives, you name it.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:55.720
 I would guess the Japanese-American population at that time was pretty tight-knit.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:01.050
 I mean, they were bound together by language and a common heritage.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:13.150
 And so they stuck together in terms of survival, but they also shared things like the food that they produced, the seeds that came from Japan that you couldn't get anyplace else.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:19.510
 And so that, in a nutshell, is how the company started.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:29.240
 We don't have a whole lot of information about what happened between then and, you know, their displacement during World War II.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.140
 There's kind of this gap.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:40.700
 We've tried to do some research and we've talked to people, relatives, but there isn't a whole lot of written information about what happened and how they survived.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:47.150
 We just heard bits and pieces that it was a struggle selling seeds.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:55.420
 They basically worked out of their, sometimes their living room, packing seeds because you get seeds in bulk.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:02.190
 You package them into smaller packages to figure out a way to distribute them or get them out to your customers.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:09.580
 It was strictly, or not say strictly, it was completely kind of a cottage industry at some level.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:19.350
 At another level, at some point, they had a storefront and there's like a picture on our wall showing what it kind of looked like in San Jose back in the day.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:28.100
 And then after World War II, the family came back to San Jose and they kind of picked up where they left off selling seeds again.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:35.160
 But again, we don't have a whole lot of information about who they are selling to or how they got their seeds.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:39.330
 It's kind of one of those mysteries that we'll never know about.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:50.860
 Mr. Kitazawa, when he was selling pre-World War II, there were a lot of Japanese-American farmers on the West Coast.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:06:04.230
 Then when World War II occurred, people lost their farms, had to either retract from the West Coast or get relocated into the camps.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:23.940
 And so in that process, two things I think happened is that after World War II, the reason why my assumptions are is that Kitazawa's seed became much smaller and hit some hard times is because people, his clientele, didn't have the farms anymore.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:37.290
 So in that process is when he was also selling throughout the West Coast rather than just on the West Coast area.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:44.160
 We have a real international audience, so we should talk a little bit about the internment.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:52.260
 And I think it's relevant to the times that we're living through now and kind of the rise of xenophobia in our country and in Europe.

00:06:53.020 --> 00:07:02.940
 There's a point where, because of World War II, the United States government decides to imprison all its Japanese residents.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:10.290
 Yeah, I think from a historical context, and I'm just talking off the top of my head.

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:25.660
 So from a historical context, you know, Japanese-Americans are looking back at the 75th anniversary of the signing of the presidential, what was it, decree?

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:37.580
 That basically allowed the U.S. government to intern Japanese-Americans to remove them from their homes, with the exception of those living in Hawaii.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:42.340
 They seem to have been somewhat allowed to stay put.

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:53.180
 But my parents, my grandparents, Maya's parents, her grandparents were all interned in various locations for a couple of years.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:55.680
 My father served in the U.S. Army.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:08:13.010
 At some point, my mother was allowed to leave the Thule Lake Camp, and she went to college back in Minnesota, where I guess it was considered she wasn't a threat to anybody being isolated in Minnesota.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:15.790
 And so she was able to go to college there.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:20.320
 And then my aunts and uncles, they were all interned as well.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:35.230
 And so it was a critical point in everybody's lives in terms of the disruption from their normal daily activities to a new life in an internment camp.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:42.590
 And even after getting out of the internment camp, things were not quite right.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:51.600
 My parents, my father came back to their home in Sacramento to find that it was basically gone.

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:57.670
 The house that was there was there, but it was in just a terrible shape.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:08:59.250
 None of their belongings were there.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.030
 So they had to start over from scratch, basically.

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:07.340
 And I think that's kind of more of the norm for most Japanese Americans.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.330
 I mean, there are some that were luckier.

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:21.810
 Perhaps their farms were taken care of by a good neighbor who basically took care of the farms when they were gone and paid the taxes and, you know, produced income while the family was gone.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:25.450
 But I think that was more of the exception rather than the rule.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:29.510
 Most people lost, you know, everything except what they could take with them.

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:30.750
 It's interesting.

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:46.090
 My paternal and maternal family were farmers on both sides and in real remote areas of California where the population was probably under 1,000.

00:09:46.090 --> 00:10:03.230
 And it's kind of interesting when you compare what occurred in very isolated rural areas in agriculture then and metropolitan areas where my parents' families were, were very isolated and very poor areas.

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:11.330
 And they, their farms were taken care of by their neighbors.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:19.080
 And their neighbors couldn't understand why they were being relocated or into these internment camps.

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:28.640
 And they promised them that they would take care of their property, pay their property tax, and so that they had something to come back to.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:35.900
 And so it was basically just everybody trying to make a living, to make a go of it.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:46.940
 And so it's kind of interesting that both sides of my family were able to come back to their farms and their farmhouses, which were taken care of by their neighbors.

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:55.320
 And I think the areas were so isolated and poor that they just couldn't understand why this was happening.

00:10:55.980 --> 00:11:03.300
 And whether it's isolation, I don't know, but anyway, both of my sides of my family were able to come back to it.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:08.140
 But like Jim said, is that when their families returned, life was not the same.

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:15.940
 There was a lot of prejudice targeted toward Japanese Americans and crops being damaged.

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:24.960
 My father and mother, when they first got married, they couldn't afford to buy their own farmland, and so they rented and leased farmland.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:39.950
 And my parents would say how sometimes their row crops in between their orchards, like watermelons or different types of melons, would get totally punctured because people didn't want them farming in their area.

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.250
 And these are in remote areas of California.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:49.100
 So they were targets, just like how, you know, the ethnicities that are targeted today.

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:55.840
 These are the isolated groups, and the prejudices are no different than versus now.

00:11:56.280 --> 00:12:03.800
 I grew up on Bainbridge Island, which is one of the infamous first sites of gathering people up to send them to internment camps.

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:09.570
 So I remember seeing some of the propaganda posters fomenting racism against Japanese folks.

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:15.550
 And so after a few years of that, you'd see people going out and puncturing melons.

00:12:15.550 --> 00:12:28.190
 Yeah, it's, you know, life has been good for us along the way until recently of hysteria happening again.

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:33.750
 And it's hard to understand that this exists.

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:39.590
 But in my parents' eyes, they truly believe, I mean, they experienced it.

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:41.410
 Very hurtful experiences.

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:42.270
 Yeah.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:49.280
 Well, I guess what you know about Kitazawa is that after World War II, they're able to restart the business.

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:54.320
 And do you know more about the company in those years after World War II?

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:58.500
 We just knew through family members that it was very lean years.

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:06.760
 And who knows what happens, you know, everybody's experiences from those camps of what it does to you personally,

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:16.640
 or how much it injures you, it just did not come back like it was, as it was prior to World War II.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:28.670
 And so I don't know what happened to the family, but they just said that the business shrank and they continued to run it out of their home.

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:35.620
 Let's talk a little bit about kind of the parallel history of when I understand that you're both gardeners

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:40.580
 and that you were using, buying Kitazawa seed in your home garden.

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:42.940
 Is that, was that your first contact with the company?

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:55.680
 Well, I used to, my being from farming families, that my grandparents taught me how to garden with Kitazawa seeds.

00:13:55.900 --> 00:14:03.270
 And I remember the manila envelope with the green ink stamp on it, and which we continue to use today.

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:11.080
 And the role of the Kitazawa seeds, historically, is huge in Japanese-American culinary.

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:13.740
 So anyway, that's how I learned.

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:16.910
 And then my parents always had gardens.

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:25.310
 I grew up on a farm, and we continue to garden that way, providing our own vegetables for our table.

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:32.350
 And then one year, my father asked, hey, I didn't get my mail order, and I didn't know what's going to happen.

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.990
 And so he asked me to go down to San Jose.

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:42.990
 And so I found out that the last person who was running the company passed away, and they didn't know what was going to happen.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:54.250
 And so Helen Komatsu was running the company at that time, and she said, well, I'll send your seeds, but she said, I don't know what's going to happen to this company.

00:14:54.450 --> 00:15:03.110
 And I felt, I did tell her that your company has such a huge part of Japanese-American history that it just can't go under.

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:15.810
 And so we had discussions about if it was going to be closed, that it would be something that I think that I would like to pursue and to continue it on as a legacy.

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:22.120
 And so it took about three or four months, and she called, and she said, I've had it.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:22.700
 I'm done.

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:31.420
 My mimeograph machine broke down, and I don't know how to produce these packets anymore.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:38.830
 So, lo and behold, we came to an agreement, and that's how we landed with Kitazawa Seed Company.

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:46.490
 So, yeah, it's been, it's a great, a great industry to be in, the seed industry.

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:48.230
 Every day is new.

00:15:48.790 --> 00:16:02.340
 There's so much change always happening, and the clientele of working with farmers, specialty growers, chefs, retailers, home gardeners across the U.S.

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:04.960
 It's been a really great experience.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:09.410
 Yeah, what were you doing for a living before you became seed sellers?

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:12.270
 What was the transition like?

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:13.510
 What was the transition?

00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:16.090
 Well, let's see.

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:18.270
 What was I doing before Kitazawa?

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:24.410
 Well, my early part of my career, I was doing market research in the housing industry.

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:42.550
 Something totally different, very analytical, very little face-to-face except with corporate-type meeting situations versus, you know, real basic food-to-table type conversations, which is so refreshing.

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:54.920
 So, that's the difference is doing market analyzing versus dealing with home gardeners and plants.

00:16:55.560 --> 00:17:02.420
 Was there a point when you thought, like, what did I get myself into in the seed industry, or has it been fun the whole time?

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:12.950
 Well, it's, over the last 20 years, it's been transitions of, when we took over the business, there was a huge learning curve.

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:14.850
 I mean, and it had to be immediate.

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:22.790
 And we also saw the shrinking clientele of the Kitazawa business.

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:25.610
 It was mostly all Japanese-Americans.

00:17:26.350 --> 00:17:31.410
 So, through that, the first three or four years, at that time, no internet.

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:42.870
 And so, I committed probably eight hours a day, five days a week, writing letters to chefs, newspapers, and magazines.

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:43.230
 Wow.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:43.910
 Okay.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:47.290
 And it was basically cold calling.

00:17:47.290 --> 00:18:01.360
 Then we were fortunate to land, gosh, within three months of taking over the business, we had a four-page layout in House and Garden Magazine, which was a national magazine.

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:03.680
 And I was like, wow, this is the best thing.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:05.180
 This is going to be so easy.

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:07.120
 Letter from Martha Stewart.

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:09.680
 I mean, this is easy.

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:14.320
 So, anyway, we had a lot of fortune along the way.

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:16.280
 That people were recognizing us.

00:18:16.280 --> 00:18:22.040
 And then the power of the internet happened, and it brought in a whole other way of doing business.

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:31.700
 And that basically also gave us some global interaction with customers that we've been working with for many years now.

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:34.020
 And so, we work with exporting.

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:46.460
 And over the last 10 years, what's been a fun part of our business and a good part of our business is to support U.S. agriculture.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:57.960
 Kitazawa seed company initially was importing all their seed, the Asian varieties, because a lot of them cannot be produced in the U.S.

00:18:57.960 --> 00:19:03.250
 And so, that was something that always bothered our philosophy.

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:09.750
 And so, we made it a decision to support U.S. agriculture.

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:16.490
 And so, now, over half of our business is selling U.S.-provided seed.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:18.800
 And so, we're doing both.

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:25.260
 We're importing and we're also exporting U.S.-grown seed and selling U.S.-grown seed in the United States.

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:30.230
 And who are the seed growers, or what does a seed-growing farm look like?

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:32.250
 Seeds are a global commodity.

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:38.130
 Seeds are essential for agricultural production across the world.

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:46.300
 And so, it's interesting even that we work primarily with, say, Japanese seed companies.

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:55.510
 These companies have had a U.S. presence for 30, 40 years because Japan's land mass is relatively small.

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:58.190
 You know, we know it's about the same size as California.

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:09.730
 And so, these seed companies have a global presence both in California, across into Oregon, Washington, Arizona, even down to Mexico.

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:18.840
 And seed companies today have a presence in the Southern Hemisphere because their seasons are opposite from ours.

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:27.730
 And so, they can grow their seeds year-round because of the North and Southern Hemispheres being opposite seasons.

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:32.350
 And it's also as an insurance to prevent, you know, crop failure.

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:39.540
 So, if a crop failure occurs in Northern Hemisphere, perhaps the Southern Hemisphere doesn't have a crop failure.

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:44.680
 And so, the seeds produced in the Southern Hemisphere will supplement the North and vice versa.

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:52.100
 And so, it's a very diverse set of circumstances that powers seed production everywhere.

00:20:52.700 --> 00:21:04.720
 And because of that, you will find, like in California, you drive down Highway 5 in an isolated part of the state, you'll see a little plot of land that's green.

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:07.240
 And it's like, what are these people doing out in the middle of nowhere?

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:21.350
 Well, odds are they're probably producing seeds because they want the seeds to be grown in an isolated area where it can't be cross-pollinated by some other pollen that gets away and contaminates the field.

00:21:21.350 --> 00:21:25.930
 So, isolation is really very important for seed production.

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:44.230
 And so, there are large areas of California and now in Oregon, Idaho, Washington that are remote where seed producers can be assured that there'll be no cross-pollination taking place that might damage or interrupt their seed production.

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:49.950
 So, that's one of the issues that we recognize is that it is a global commodity.

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:53.600
 Seeds are being produced here in California.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:56.980
 They're shipped to Japan where it might be further processed.

00:21:57.700 --> 00:22:04.480
 They get shipped back to us where we repackage them and sell them and vice versa.

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:06.680
 Lots of seeds are produced here in the U.S.

00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:13.520
 They package, sent to Japan, sent to China, sent all over the world to feed the world.

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:16.360
 So, it's an interesting process to watch.

00:22:16.360 --> 00:22:30.780
 It's pretty amazing how much we rely on others to produce our seeds, not just other countries, but other businesses in terms of the global reach and how important it is to have these global connections.

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:44.950
 And again, it comes back to the whole notion that for all this to work, we have to work together with other producing countries in order for this to all make sense and to pencil out financially.

00:22:44.950 --> 00:23:03.050
 And as soon as you start talking about tariffs or political exercises to try and change the way things are, it does have an influence on some basic things like what we eat, what's it going to cost for us to produce food, who's going to buy our food, who are we going to sell our food to.

00:23:03.370 --> 00:23:09.030
 I mean, it gets very complicated because it's all part of this bigger global economy.

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:18.900
 Also, the seed production is also influenced by labor costs because some seed production takes a lot of manual labor.

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:29.660
 And so, you'll find that regions where seed production occurs internationally will also follow those economic patterns of where seed will be produced.

00:23:29.660 --> 00:23:39.530
 I mentioned before we started recording that my mom used to buy pitazawa seeds and varieties like red Russian kale and mizuna, things that at that time you couldn't find anywhere else.

00:23:39.790 --> 00:23:41.630
 Of course, you can find those everywhere now.

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:49.900
 By maintaining this global network of providers and of customers, there's access to this incredible amount of biodiversity.

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:58.120
 There's, you know, as I said, that there's a lot of species that we offer that cannot be produced in the United States.

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:03.860
 And the culinary scene in the United States has made them very popular.

00:24:03.860 --> 00:24:12.690
 So, what we do is we search for those rare open pollinate varieties that a lot of people are asking us to find.

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:27.680
 And we work with seed companies in Japan that search for small productions or look for species for us because they know what we do here in the United States and they want to assist us in bringing unique seeds.

00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:35.490
 So, one seed that we've carried for 100 years is the shishito pepper.

00:24:35.490 --> 00:24:46.920
 And now I see it sold at Trader Joe's and Safeway and served as an app at the Cheesecake Factory.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:55.110
 And it seems like it took 100 years to bring it to the American table.

00:24:56.370 --> 00:25:05.740
 And just the other day, I mean, a big farmer said, oh, a chain store wants me to grow this shishito pepper.

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:07.680
 So, what is this shishito pepper?

00:25:08.060 --> 00:25:09.580
 What makes it so special?

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:12.480
 So, anyway, it was new to him.

00:25:12.760 --> 00:25:15.780
 And so, I had to explain to him how long it had existed.

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:20.380
 And he's like, he couldn't believe it that it was under the radar for this long.

00:25:21.190 --> 00:25:35.450
 But we have some old heirlooms here that are still are not mainstream here, but have existed so long in Asia that they're daily products for us.

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:44.260
 I mean, we carry things like the Kamo eggplant, which is what we call a dento yasai of Japan.

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:50.980
 It's like a treasured variety, maybe equivalent to what we call here an heirloom.

00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:54.540
 We have shishigatani squash.

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:56.200
 It's a Japanese pumpkin.

00:25:57.160 --> 00:25:58.620
 The manganji pepper.

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:12.040
 That's an old variety that is not even, I mean, people, I think, who are into the Asian culinary scene or have traveled can identify with it.

00:26:12.040 --> 00:26:16.620
 Or chefs that have, like, seriously studied cuisine in Asia might know of it.

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:28.580
 But one of our main purposes here is to provide these specialty varieties that are known in other parts of the world, but not here.

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:31.700
 So, we're trying to still get the word out.

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:35.920
 Do you go out searching for vegetable varieties?

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:36.800
 That sounds amazing.

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:37.500
 We do.

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:46.460
 That's something that our relationships globally, we've been working with them for a long time.

00:26:47.290 --> 00:26:52.830
 And prior to our ownership, we've still been maintaining those relationships.

00:26:53.870 --> 00:27:04.460
 And so, they constantly have their ears open or evaluate what we have, our offering here, and we'll make suggestions to it.

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:11.480
 And because there's all these different little regions throughout Asia that have these specialty varieties.

00:27:11.860 --> 00:27:12.220
 Yeah.

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:16.300
 What are some examples of recent ones or ones that you would like to carry?

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:23.960
 We just started to import Chinese cabbages that were developed in Korea specifically for making kimchi.

00:27:24.590 --> 00:27:27.430
 And so, a lot of our customers are interested in making kimchi.

00:27:28.690 --> 00:27:34.930
 And so, what's different about these varieties from Korea is that they have a lower moisture content.

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:48.290
 And so, when you're making kimchi, you want kind of a drier Chinese cabbage, so you don't have to use as much salt to draw out all the fluid that's in the leaves and not affect the flavor.

00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:53.580
 So, that's an example of something that, you know, the Koreans have been making kimchi forever.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:28:07.950
 And so, selling a Korean variety of Chinese cabbage that's for the kimchi market is perfect for people who want to experiment with kimchi and to kind of replicate what is done in Korea.

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:16.970
 It's also interesting that it wasn't too long ago that, you know, we didn't have refrigeration when you think about it.

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:18.880
 We used ice boxes.

00:28:19.220 --> 00:28:21.460
 And prior to ice boxes, guess what?

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:23.240
 There wasn't refrigeration.

00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:25.000
 All of our foods were pickled.

00:28:25.450 --> 00:28:27.420
 They were salted, pickled, dried.

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:30.080
 And that's how we survived.

00:28:30.680 --> 00:28:40.800
 And it's interesting for me to think about the history of man not too long ago not having refrigeration.

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:49.610
 And a good portion of the world today still do not have refrigeration, yet they're able to eat because they're preserving foods in a very traditional way.

00:28:49.610 --> 00:28:53.990
 On the flip side, we're being told salt is bad for us.

00:28:54.130 --> 00:28:58.150
 We eat too much salt and it creates high blood pressure, etc.

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:06.310
 Yet, at the same time, mankind has been using salt to preserve foods for as long as we've been walking on this earth.

00:29:06.530 --> 00:29:15.880
 So, there's a bit of a contradiction in terms of how much salt we eat versus how much salt we should be using to preserve our food because that's the way we have evolved.

00:29:16.220 --> 00:29:25.360
 And instead, we're looking at using food preservatives that none of us can pronounce, let alone even understand how it works.

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:40.660
 And so, we live in a complicated world where we're being told salt is bad for us, yet at the same time, we've been pickling our vegetables for eons using salt, basically, and vinegar in simple food processes.

00:29:41.020 --> 00:29:51.150
 I come back to this notion that many of the varieties that are produced in Asia and in Japan were produced specifically for pickling.

00:29:51.430 --> 00:29:51.650
 Why?

00:29:51.770 --> 00:30:03.340
 Because they had really distinctive flavors that you could add a lot of salt to it, you could pickle it, and the flavor still comes out through all of this, you know, processing and preservation.

00:30:03.340 --> 00:30:13.590
 And that's an important thing to think about for small producers or even home gardeners who want to, you know, capture the best flavor.

00:30:13.910 --> 00:30:14.930
 You can do that.

00:30:15.230 --> 00:30:17.610
 And if you're into pickling, because guess what?

00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:23.110
 When you have a small garden and you produce all these vegetables, you can't possibly eat them all at once.

00:30:23.310 --> 00:30:30.760
 You have to either give them away, refrigerate them, which we don't have refrigerators big enough, or you pickle them.

00:30:30.940 --> 00:30:32.100
 You can them or pickle them.

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:34.410
 And that's the way of the world.

00:30:34.550 --> 00:30:38.050
 I mean, that's how people have been doing it for long periods of time.

00:30:38.130 --> 00:30:38.830
 They're still pickling.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:47.450
 And it's just ironic and perhaps coincidental that there's a growing interest today in people learning how to pickle.

00:30:47.830 --> 00:30:48.710
 And it's like, really?

00:30:49.190 --> 00:30:50.810
 I thought everybody knew how to pickle.

00:30:52.910 --> 00:31:07.150
 But if you've lived in urban life, you know, for generations, and you don't have access to fresh vegetables, and you don't know how to pickle, I mean, it's just the way things are.

00:31:07.490 --> 00:31:19.880
 And so, there's new interest in basic food preservation and trying to come up with new ways, not new ways, but new flavors of pickling.

00:31:19.960 --> 00:31:22.480
 And you're seeing that also in restaurants as well.

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:23.480
 People are experimenting.

00:31:23.720 --> 00:31:30.990
 They're putting together different combinations of vegetables and spices to pickle things and serve them up.

00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:31.870
 And guess what?

00:31:31.930 --> 00:31:33.410
 It doesn't require refrigeration.

00:31:33.510 --> 00:31:34.430
 It stores well.

00:31:34.790 --> 00:31:36.210
 It's relatively inexpensive.

00:31:36.830 --> 00:31:39.210
 And how much more basic can it get?

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:41.650
 It's just salt, water, and vegetables.

00:31:42.130 --> 00:31:45.290
 And there's the whole microbiome research area, right?

00:31:45.470 --> 00:31:47.250
 It just sounds like it's really good for you, too.

00:31:47.530 --> 00:31:47.930
 Exactly.

00:31:48.010 --> 00:31:56.910
 That's a whole different conversation about how fermented foods and preserving foods in a natural way is good for our digestive systems.

00:31:57.370 --> 00:31:59.380
 And it's beneficial.

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:07.000
 And so, there are these elements that I think we've lost because we're no longer an agrarian society.

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:09.210
 We're a technology society.

00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:12.110
 And so, things have gotten lost during that time.

00:32:12.250 --> 00:32:27.000
 And I think we're in a position where we can sell seeds to people who are interested in kind of relearning things that they may have known or their ancestors certainly knew, but have been forgotten.

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:33.420
 It's a lost art in terms of pickling and preserving and saving your vegetables.

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:37.260
 We sell a lot of kabocha squash, Japanese squash.

00:32:37.760 --> 00:32:50.170
 And that's an example of something that as long as you store it in a cool, dry place, the squash will stay good months without any refrigeration, without any sort of, you know, preservatives.

00:32:50.410 --> 00:32:52.680
 And it was designed that way.

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:54.000
 It has a tough skin.

00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:59.100
 It can withstand, you know, long periods of time without losing its flavor.

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:04.780
 And when you cut it open and cook it, it tastes as good as it does back when it was first harvested.

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:11.340
 So, there's some interesting flavors that are inherent in some of the vegetables that we're selling today.

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:17.670
 That they do have a long history of being flavorful over the course of time.

00:33:17.670 --> 00:33:25.360
 So, we have two pepper seeds that migrated here from South Korea through Namu Farm, Kristen Leach.

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:27.120
 Did you talk about that?

00:33:27.320 --> 00:33:29.760
 Yeah, we, well, we did a little bit on the interview.

00:33:29.860 --> 00:33:31.740
 Yeah, Kristen, we interviewed for the last season.

00:33:31.940 --> 00:33:33.120
 She actually is an incredible farmer.

00:33:33.280 --> 00:33:36.920
 And you work with her on a line of seeds called Second Generation Seeds?

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:37.200
 Correct.

00:33:37.620 --> 00:33:38.000
 Yes.

00:33:38.480 --> 00:33:52.040
 And we currently, the first two varieties that we are selling of Kristen's or Namu Farm is two peppers for gogujang seasoning.

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:57.770
 And Kristen found these seeds or were given these seeds by a hermit lady.

00:33:57.770 --> 00:34:11.930
 And to pay homage to this hermit lady that we were sitting around the table one day thinking, how are we going to name these for her?

00:34:12.410 --> 00:34:16.850
 And so, we named them Lady Choi and Lady Hermit.

00:34:16.850 --> 00:34:29.240
 And from day one, they've been, it's the interest of, they were on our cover catalog and been phenomenal sales for us.

00:34:29.540 --> 00:34:35.600
 And people's interest and reception to pickling or Korean cooking.

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:40.300
 And so, we feel honored to sell that for Namu Farm.

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:43.430
 Well, let's talk some more about that second generation seeds.

00:34:43.430 --> 00:34:47.790
 Just to tell us a quick story, we interviewed Kristen late fall last year.

00:34:48.290 --> 00:34:53.090
 And I did that when our new baby is just a month old.

00:34:53.910 --> 00:35:02.450
 And so, she gave me some leftover seedlings of a, oh, it was a Korean mustard for a kimchi, right?

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:04.710
 This little six-pack of ones.

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:08.610
 And I brought it home and just neglected it because we had a new child.

00:35:08.610 --> 00:35:14.490
 Our whole garden got neglected and I left that six-pack out over the winter.

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:20.590
 And the mustard put down roots through the, broke open the plastic.

00:35:21.050 --> 00:35:25.640
 I know it's six feet high, these huge red leaves.

00:35:26.210 --> 00:35:34.140
 But Kristen's been growing things to be these strong, independent, tough plants adapted to California.

00:35:34.140 --> 00:35:46.060
 Yeah, it's amazing what she's doing with some of these, adapting, not just importing seeds, but adapting them to both this place and her vision of how farming can work.

00:35:46.060 --> 00:35:52.560
 She adapted the lady peppers for four years before releasing them.

00:35:53.280 --> 00:36:05.190
 And so, we feel positive, very positive about how they're going to acclimate or work throughout U.S. for home gardeners and for commercial growers.

00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:15.150
 So, and the whole rise of Korean cooking or cuisine in the United States is really taking on strong.

00:36:15.150 --> 00:36:21.430
 I can't imagine that the growing climate of South Korea is very similar to the East Bay Hills or Rishi Farms.

00:36:22.300 --> 00:36:25.900
 So, it's a big project to adapt vegetables to their new homes.

00:36:25.900 --> 00:36:40.430
 I generally feel that the weather here in California, our soil, our access to some of the best water resources, makes it an ideal place to grow vegetables and fruit, for that matter.

00:36:40.990 --> 00:37:00.760
 And so, I think that if you have a plot of land here in California that's reasonably balanced in terms of nutrients and relatively deep, as you might find in certain parts of the Central Valley, and access to good water, it's remarkable what is being produced in California.

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:08.390
 So, a lot of produce from all over the world seems to thrive in California.

00:37:08.870 --> 00:37:18.700
 And I think all you have to do is go into, like, Chinatown and walk through some of the stalls where they're selling just all sorts of vegetables.

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:20.180
 Some I can't even identify.

00:37:20.990 --> 00:37:23.700
 And they're probably grown not too far from here.

00:37:24.280 --> 00:37:28.220
 And it's all, quote, locally grown and enjoyed by many.

00:37:28.360 --> 00:37:34.320
 I guess all kinds of people have been bringing seeds with them to California for longer than even Kitazawa's been around.

00:37:36.140 --> 00:37:44.210
 Yeah, there are some, there's a specific variety, Aquatica ipomea, that is a water spinach.

00:37:44.690 --> 00:37:55.410
 And you can go down to Chinatown or Asian markets in this metropolitan area and find it in almost every store during the specific season.

00:37:55.690 --> 00:37:59.130
 We get customers asking us and farmers, where can we get the seed?

00:37:59.630 --> 00:38:04.330
 But it is illegal to import the seed.

00:38:05.110 --> 00:38:09.330
 And so, people are wondering, well, how do they get these seeds?

00:38:09.830 --> 00:38:17.210
 But, you know, I mean, it's considered an invasive species, but they're not growing it near waterways.

00:38:17.570 --> 00:38:24.270
 And it's considered, in certain parts of Asia, an essential green in their diet.

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:28.510
 So, they miss it and they find ways to bring it here.

00:38:29.690 --> 00:38:37.120
 I'm going to ask you about where people can find seeds and follow along with what you're doing and all that kind of thing.

00:38:37.180 --> 00:38:45.830
 But before I do that, I just wanted to ask if there's any other aspect of the seed company or what you're doing that we haven't talked about that you want to discuss.

00:38:47.110 --> 00:39:07.150
 One of the things, through dumb luck on our part, as well as the power of the internet, the combination of our dumb luck in deciding to create a website so that we could attempt to sell seeds over the internet from a business standpoint was a critical move.

00:39:07.150 --> 00:39:13.010
 It's because Kitazawa seed company was primarily a mail-order catalog type of business.

00:39:13.770 --> 00:39:16.800
 And we continue to produce a catalog today.

00:39:17.100 --> 00:39:21.780
 We send out upwards of 75,000 catalogs every year.

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:23.350
 There's still interest.

00:39:23.470 --> 00:39:25.670
 People still request catalogs from us.

00:39:26.150 --> 00:39:30.690
 We get compliments for our catalog because of the information that's in it.

00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:39.180
 But from a purely business perspective, the internet itself has been a tremendous boost to us.

00:39:39.360 --> 00:39:45.190
 We've been able to reach customers who continue to find us today through the internet search.

00:39:45.350 --> 00:39:46.770
 They contact us.

00:39:46.830 --> 00:39:48.050
 They find out more about us.

00:39:48.110 --> 00:39:52.730
 They can read about Asian vegetables, the seeds that we sell online.

00:39:53.050 --> 00:39:54.530
 They can order online.

00:39:55.170 --> 00:39:59.390
 It just has changed the dynamics of the whole business.

00:40:00.070 --> 00:40:06.090
 And so that, I think, has been a real important element to our business.

00:40:06.090 --> 00:40:14.530
 And it also changes how we communicate and work with our customers because everything is so instantaneous.

00:40:14.870 --> 00:40:19.270
 We find out, you know, immediately if we sent out the wrong seeds.

00:40:19.590 --> 00:40:27.910
 We find out immediately or within like two or three weeks if the seeds didn't germinate and they're having a problem with, you know, a variety or something.

00:40:28.150 --> 00:40:29.070
 It's not by telephone.

00:40:29.230 --> 00:40:31.510
 It's all by email that comes in.

00:40:31.590 --> 00:40:34.210
 And so that has changed how we work.

00:40:34.210 --> 00:40:42.570
 It means on weekends when home gardeners are trying to decide which seeds to buy, they're shopping.

00:40:43.230 --> 00:40:46.450
 Saturdays and Sundays are big shopping days for home gardeners.

00:40:46.550 --> 00:40:49.470
 And so it means Monday we have to be ready to work.

00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:58.410
 We're always looking for that special vegetable for people to experience.

00:40:58.890 --> 00:41:21.670
 And whether in an heirloom or new breeding programs by our companies that we represent, one of the special items that we have been able to sample but not able to market very well because of the cost of production and breeding is the red Chinese cabbage.

00:41:21.670 --> 00:41:31.030
 And it's a full-headed, barrelhead type Chinese cabbage that has red leaves.

00:41:31.510 --> 00:41:33.450
 And it's absolutely stunning.

00:41:34.130 --> 00:41:37.350
 And we tested it in our garden.

00:41:38.050 --> 00:41:44.120
 And it seemed like it was the only thing that the rats liked to eat.

00:41:44.340 --> 00:41:45.760
 So we knew it was good.

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:51.100
 And they would just keep on going after it as new growth came out.

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:59.300
 But anyway, there are a lot of challenges that are occurring in agriculture that we can introduce new things.

00:41:59.300 --> 00:42:17.690
 But what people are willing to pay for the R&D in a breeding program where seed companies put a lot of time, money, energy into breeding programs.

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:22.400
 And so, you know, they also want to make it a hit.

00:42:22.660 --> 00:42:25.660
 But how many of those are actually going to be successful?

00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:32.080
 So anyway, we're trying to always find and provide new and interesting varieties for our customers.

00:42:32.520 --> 00:42:34.060
 And we work really hard at that.

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:38.860
 Your customers, it's really different than when Kitazawa started out 100 years ago, right?

00:42:38.900 --> 00:42:41.380
 And has that changed recently with the internet?

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:43.460
 I know you said that it's a broader area.

00:42:43.640 --> 00:42:53.880
 Yeah, our customer base from when we first started was, well, when we first started, it was probably 95% Japanese clientele.

00:42:54.740 --> 00:42:57.180
 I mean, you could just see the database names.

00:42:58.760 --> 00:43:06.740
 And then over the years, we had to transition that in order to stay in business.

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:13.230
 So, you know, the power of the internet and the popularity of Asian vegetables.

00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:21.670
 We have been able to transition it, not only Japanese Americans, but all ethnicities.

00:43:21.830 --> 00:43:27.570
 And there's a huge immigration that occurred in the United States since the time that we have done.

00:43:28.230 --> 00:43:33.730
 And so they have brought whole new recommended vegetables to us.

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:39.190
 Like Cho here at the office, she introduced us, and Kristen did, to sour leaf.

00:43:39.530 --> 00:43:46.870
 Kristen was growing it for the hibiscus flower for the buds.

00:43:47.770 --> 00:43:50.070
 And Cho introduced it to us.

00:43:50.230 --> 00:43:51.030
 She's from Burma.

00:43:51.730 --> 00:43:55.550
 And in their culinary cooking, they use it, the leaves.

00:43:56.210 --> 00:43:59.450
 So just totally what Kristen was growing it for the buds.

00:43:59.890 --> 00:44:12.560
 And it is an incredible, incredible vegetable that I don't think people in the U.S. do not even know what this is, this vegetable is like.

00:44:13.020 --> 00:44:24.080
 My experience and palate with it is like eating a spinach that tastes like umeboshi, but naturally, not with a lot of salt.

00:44:24.790 --> 00:44:26.250
 I mean, it's not pickled or anything.

00:44:26.730 --> 00:44:36.460
 So if you can only imagine that with traditional spices with turmeric and paprika, it just takes it in chili.

00:44:36.760 --> 00:44:45.780
 So we were really excited to find the seed, bring it here, and we were surprised in how much we were moving out now.

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:47.760
 I think that's a great place to leave it.

00:44:47.920 --> 00:44:56.100
 And before we stop recording it, just will you say the website name and where people can find the seeds and also kind of know more about Kitazawa?

00:44:56.100 --> 00:45:00.820
 Yes, we can be found at KitazawaSeed.com.

00:45:01.520 --> 00:45:08.800
 And we accept orders by mail, the old-fashioned way, phone, and through our website.

00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:09.960
 Well, thanks so much.

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:15.800
 It's been a pleasure to talk to you and see where all these packets come through when you send out all these orders.

00:45:16.740 --> 00:45:17.600
 Thank you, Devin.

